  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 404 AM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe wavy west-to-east quasi-stationary front referenced in \nprevious discussions sill remains draped over the Central Plains\ninto the mid-Mississippi River Valley and southern Great Lakes.\nWeak high pressure remains to the north of this front, \nessentially sitting atop MN/WI through the day today. This will \nresult in dry but continued warm conditions today with highs \ninto the 80s area- wide with a mix of sun and clouds. Clouds \nincrease tonight as a low- level trough feature, emanating from \na low pressure center along the front over the Plains, extends \nnortheast into the Upper Midwest. Once again, enhanced low-level\nnocturnal jetting interacting with this trough within a \ncontinued moist environment (low 70s dewpoints again expected in\nfar western into far southern MN) will spark off scattered \nshowers/thunderstorms from this evening through daybreak \nThursday morning. The focus area of convection will be far \nwestern through far southern MN, in closer proximity to the \nomnipresent surface front and its associated trough. Storms look\nto develop in eastern SD and drive southeast, potentially in \nenough of an organized fashion where a few storms could be \nstrong to severe late tonight into early Thursday morning. The \nSPC SWODY1 does a good job of focusing in the Marginal Risk of \nthat potential, while also indicating that the better chances \nfor higher concentration/intensity of storms will be to the \nsouth of the WFO MPX coverage area.\n\nA small airmass change looks to occur starting on Thursday as  \nnorthwest flow takes a better hold over the region and modified \ncolder Canadian high pressure makes inroads from the northwest. \nThis will start a period of highs dropping into the 70s area-\nwide and lows in the 50s outside of the Twin Cities metro (near \n60 within). There is decent agreement on an organized area of \nlow pressure arriving from the southwest Friday into Saturday, \ndragging a slug of moisture from the Deep South into the Upper \nMidwest as it arrives. This looks to result in fairly widespread\nrainfall from late Friday through much of Saturday in the form \nof more stratiform rain rather than convection. That said, model\nguidance has tweaked down the overall QPF of the system, \nranging from around a half inch in the Twin Cities metro to \ncloser to an inch near the IA border and into southwest WI from \nFriday through Saturday. Still not expecting much in the way of \nthunderstorm activity but a few embedded t-storms are certainly \npossible. \n\nTemperatures overall look to remain fairly steady from late this \nweek into early next week, with the north-central CONUS \nremaining under the influence of northwest flow. There are still\nsome signs of a western ridge developing late next week which \ncould result in an appreciable warming for the Upper Midwest. \nHowever, overall blended guidance through the next 7 days starts\nout with temperatures above normal today then dropping slightly\nbelow normal from Friday through the middle of next week.",
